In this lesson we'll look at how ratios help us compare quantities and share things fairly. A ratio just tells you how much of one thing there is compared to another. For example, a ratio of one to four means for every one of the first thing, there are four of the second.

There are two kinds to know. Part-to-part compares one piece with another, like two cups of flour to one cup of sugar in a recipe, giving a ratio of two to one. Part-to-whole compares one piece with everything combined, so the flour is two parts out of three total, giving a ratio of two to three.

You can write ratios in a few ways, like two to three, or as a fraction with two parts. To simplify a ratio, divide every number by their highest common factor, so six to nine becomes two to three.

The real power move is finding the unit value, which is what one part is worth. Just divide the total by the number of parts. So if you're sharing forty-five dollars in the ratio two to three, that's five parts, giving nine dollars per part, so the first share is eighteen dollars and the second is twenty-seven.

Quick warning though, order matters, so one to two is very different from two to one. Now let's see it in action.